-- DICT-SEMANTICS §3 W3 (method-scheme fidelity), PINNED-TYPE axis -- the sibling
-- of s3-w3-method-scheme-rigidity-constraint with the constraint route removed.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): the body returns `K`, a nullary constructor of a
-- user data type with NO class constraint anywhere. So the "needs a constraint
-- the scheme lacks" test cannot fire; what W3 forbids here is the bare act of
-- pinning: "`b̄ μ̄` belong to the CALLER -- `var` re-instantiates them freshly at
-- every use site -- so a body that pins one inhabits a strictly more specific
-- type than the scheme every call site is entitled to". REJECT.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all REJECT with `T-IMPL-TOO-SPECIFIC`
-- ("... this body fixes them to specific types"). CONFORMANT.
--
-- Why BOTH W3 rows: an implementation that only checked "did the body acquire a
-- constraint the scheme lacks" would pass the constraint row and silently accept
-- this one, which is the unsound half (§3 W3 "type axis": `mk : a -> b` with a
-- body returning 42 fixes `b := Int`; a caller instantiating `b := String`
-- crashes). Checking the SET, not one member.
data K = K

interface Mk a where
  mkv : a -> b

impl Mk Int where
  mkv _ = K

main = println (debug (mkv (1 : Int) : K))
